<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William de Braose, 4th Lord of Bramber</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1144–1211</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William de Braose, 4th Lord of Bramber, was a powerful Anglo-Norman marcher lord in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> who held Brecknock through his mother and received Gower from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> in 1203. Notorious for the massacre of Welsh chiefs at Abergavenny in 1175, he later fell from John's favour and died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-de-braose-4th-lord-of-bramber.jpg" alt="William de Braose, 4th Lord of Bramber" width="320" height="351" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_de_Braose,_4th_Lord_Bramber.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
